 **Step 3 of the one-refiner plan (built on a branch, live after merge and a Servex restart):** `session.coverage()` (`core/Session/Session.js`) counts the owner's sentences in a session and how many a task cites; the session page shows it as one line under the stats (`ai/AISession/SessionStats.js`), with the uncited sentences one click down. It is the number the next steps move. It counts sentences with the browser splitter (`core/Task/sentences.js`), which does not redact or quick-clean, so it reads about 8% higher than the study's `Ask.split` count (131 against 121 on session 5132a480).
 
+**Steps 4 and 5 of the one-refiner plan (built on a branch, live after merge and a Servex restart):** which channel refines, and where. **VS Code tab:** Echo writes the sections, and `PromptTasks` (`Servex/agents/PromptTasks.js`, only for an owner's `vscode-tab` session) runs the `live` process for the parts. **Web dictation:** `Sessions.refine` (`Servex/agents/Sessions.js`) writes the sections. **Cloud voice:** had no refiner; `RemoteMCP.record` (`Servex/ext/remote/RemoteMCP.js`) now calls `TurnRefiner.refine` (`Servex/agents/TurnRefiner.js`) right after `Ask.write`. It runs the same parts brief through `PromptRecords.parts_of` (the model call factored out of `parts_one`, so no second prompt record is written) and appends a second `refined` line, same `re`, whose sections cite the turn's sentences. Proof on a scratch turn of 4 sentences: cited 0 before, 4 after, $0.008 on Sonnet. Detail: [prompt-records.md](/framework/ext/Refine/doc/prompt-records.md#channels-and-the-turn-refiner).
